---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Tldr from '../../components/Tldr.astro';
import KeyTakeaways from '../../components/KeyTakeaways.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/CopyPrompt.astro';
import TryItNow from '../../components/TryItNow.astro';
import FeatureCard from '../../components/FeatureCard.astro';
import GlossaryLink from '../../components/GlossaryLink.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Memory & Projects"
  description="Your context window resets every session. Memory and Projects are how Claude carries what matters from one conversation to the next — so you stop re-explaining yourself."
  pageId="memory"
  readMinutes={6}
>
  <PageHeader
    icon="💾"
    title="Memory & Projects"
    subtitle="Your context window resets every session. Memory and Projects are how Claude carries what matters from one conversation to the next — so you stop re-explaining yourself."
    readMinutes={6}
  />

  <Tldr>
    <p>
      The <GlossaryLink term="context-window">context window</GlossaryLink>
      is short-term memory — one session, then gone. To carry things across chats,
      Claude has two tools: <strong>Memory</strong> (it automatically remembers
      facts about you and your work) and <strong>Projects</strong> (persistent
      workspaces that hold your instructions and reference files). Add
      <strong>files and skills</strong> for durable outputs and repeatable
      workflows, and you've got a real memory system.
    </p>
  </Tldr>

  <!-- Short vs long term -->
  <section class="prose-content">
    <h2>Short-term vs. long-term</h2>
    <p>
      Think of the context window as Claude's desk: everything you're actively
      working on is spread out on it during a session — and it's cleared when you
      leave. That's perfect for the task at hand, but it means a brand-new
      conversation starts with a blank desk. <strong>Memory and Projects are the
      filing cabinet</strong> — where the important stuff lives between sessions so
      you're not starting from scratch every time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Memory -->
  <section class="prose-content">
    <h2>Memory: Claude remembers you</h2>
    <p>
      Claude can automatically build up a memory of the things that matter about
      you — your role, your preferences, the projects you keep coming back to, how
      you like things formatted. It surfaces that context in future conversations,
      so you don't have to reintroduce yourself or re-explain your job every time.
      It quietly improves the more you work together.
    </p>
    <p>
      Crucially, memory is <strong>scoped</strong>: each Project keeps its own
      separate memory, so your "supplier onboarding" context doesn't bleed into
      your "board deck" context.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="🔒 You're in control">
    <p>
      You can review, edit, and clear what Claude remembers — or turn memory off
      entirely. And the same rule from <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/safety/')}>Safety &amp; Data</a>
      still applies: don't hand Claude passwords, bulk PII, or NDA material just
      because memory is convenient. Memory is for working context, not a vault for
      secrets.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Projects -->
  <section class="prose-content">
    <h2>Projects: a workspace that remembers</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong><GlossaryLink term="project">Project</GlossaryLink></strong> is a persistent workspace inside the Claude apps.
      You set it up once with two things:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Instructions</strong> — who you are, the tone and format you want, the rules that should apply to every chat in the workspace.</li>
      <li><strong>Knowledge files</strong> — the specs, templates, guidelines, or data Claude should always be able to reference.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      From then on, <em>every conversation inside that Project inherits the
      context automatically</em>. You skip the setup and go straight to the
      question — and Claude pulls the relevant bits of your uploaded files into the
      conversation as needed. (Projects live in Co-Work — see
      <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/modes/')}>The Three Modes</a>.)
    </p>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="cowork" title="🏭 An Aptera example">
    <p>
      Spin up a <strong>"Supplier Onboarding"</strong> Project. Drop in the W-9
      template, the Aptera Supplier Questionnaire, and the quality-cert
      requirements, and set the instruction: <em>"You help onboard new suppliers;
      keep a professional-but-warm tone."</em> Now every onboarding email you draft
      in that Project already knows the deliverables and the voice — no re-pasting,
      every time.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Files & skills -->
  <section class="prose-content">
    <h2>Files &amp; skills: the durable layer</h2>
    <p>
      For things that must truly last, reach past Claude's built-in memory:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Save outputs to files.</strong> A finished doc, runbook, or config belongs in a file (or Confluence/SharePoint), not buried in a chat that you might lose.</li>
      <li><strong>Build a skill</strong> for any repeatable, multi-step workflow you run again and again — see <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/connectors/')}>Connectors &amp; Skills</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="code" title="⌨️ For Claude Code users">
    <p>
      In Claude Code, persistent instructions and project facts live in files
      (like a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or a memory directory) committed alongside the
      code — so every session starts already knowing the project's conventions.
      Same idea as Projects, version-controlled.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Which to use -->
  <section class="prose-content reveal">
    <h2>Which kind of memory do I use?</h2>
    <p>Four layers, from most temporary to most durable:</p>
  </section>

  <div class="my-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 reveal">
    <FeatureCard emoji="🪟" title="Context window" accent="chat">
      <p>One conversation. Automatic and finite — it resets when the session ends.</p>
    </FeatureCard>
    <FeatureCard emoji="💾" title="Memory" accent="gold">
      <p>Facts about you and your work, carried across all your chats. Automatic — and you can edit or clear it.</p>
    </FeatureCard>
    <FeatureCard emoji="🗂️" title="Projects" accent="cowork">
      <p>A body of work. Your instructions + reference files, shared by every chat in the workspace.</p>
    </FeatureCard>
    <FeatureCard emoji="🛠️" title="Files & skills" accent="code">
      <p>Durable outputs and repeatable workflows — saved on purpose, reused on demand.</p>
    </FeatureCard>
  </div>

  <TryItNow title="Set up your first Project">
    <p>
      In the Claude app, create a Project for something you do repeatedly. Add any
      templates or reference docs, then paste this as the Project instructions —
      fill in the brackets:
    </p>
    <CopyPrompt text="You help me with [initiative / recurring task]. Background: [what it is, who's involved, the key constraints]. Always [tone / format rules]. Use the files in this project as the source of truth for [specs / templates / data]. Assume this context in every chat — don't make me re-explain it." />
  </TryItNow>

  <KeyTakeaways>
    <ul>
      <li>The context window is short-term (one session); Memory and Projects are how Claude remembers across sessions.</li>
      <li><strong>Memory</strong> automatically carries facts about you and your work between chats — and you can review, edit, or turn it off.</li>
      <li><strong>Projects</strong> are persistent workspaces: set instructions + add knowledge files once, and every chat inherits them.</li>
      <li>For anything that must truly last, save it to files or build a skill.</li>
      <li>Memory is for working context, not secrets — the same Safety rules apply.</li>
    </ul>
  </KeyTakeaways>
</BaseLayout>
